| `src/routes/(public)/_guest/two-factor/-hooks/use-two-factor-completion.ts` | What both forms do on success and on error |

The TanStack Router plugin adds the two routes to `src/routeTree.gen.ts` the next
time `vite` runs (`dev` or `build`). Type-check after that.

## How the flow works

1. `useTwoFactorLogin` posts the credentials to `{{ loginEndpoint }}` through the shared
   `api` (after `ensureCsrf()`). A normal login drops any earlier challenge from the store,
   fetches the current user again and then runs the login form's own `onSuccess`, exactly
   like `useLogin`.
2. A challenge response (`200` with `token_type` `verification_required` or
   `setup_required`) skips the form's `onSuccess`: the token goes into the in-memory
   store and the user is sent to `/two-factor` or `/two-factor/setup`, keeping the
   `redirect` search param. The token never goes into the URL, `localStorage` or
   `sessionStorage`; a page reload drops it and the user signs in again. `mutateAsync`
   routes a challenge the same way and resolves with it (`null` after a normal login).
3. Both screens redirect to `/login` when the store holds no unexpired token of the
   right type. "Back to sign in" clears the store.
4. The setup screen calls `setupTwoFactor` once per token. `useTwoFactorSetup` is a
   query with `staleTime: Infinity` and `retry: false`, so StrictMode's double mount
   does not generate a second secret. Its key (`["twoFactorSetup", token]`) sits outside
   the `auth` query keys, so invalidating `["auth"]` does not post `2fa/setup` again and
   rotate the secret behind the QR code. `gcTime: 0` drops the secret and the recovery
   codes from the cache as soon as the screen unmounts.
5. On success (code or recovery code) the backend creates the cookie session and
   returns the user. The screen fetches the current-user query again, navigates to
   `redirect` (or `/`), clears the store and removes the setup query.
6. When a code submission fails: `401` means the token expired or is no longer valid, so
   the store is cleared and the user goes back to `/login`. A wrong code (`422`) or a
   lockout (`429`) shows the error under the code field and keeps the user on the screen.
   Any other failure (a `500`, a network error) shows a generic error toast.
7. When the setup screen can't load (`2fa/setup` fails), it shows an error. Both screens
   always show the "Back to sign in" link.

## Manual step: use the 2FA-aware login hook

`login()` returns `void`, so the template's `useLogin` never sees a challenge. The
package never edits your files, so swap the hook yourself in
`src/routes/(public)/_guest/login/-components/login-form.tsx`:

1. Remove this import:

   ```ts
   import { useLogin } from "@/services/auth/actions";
   ```

2. Add this import right after `import { handleAxiosFieldErrors, redirectTarget } from "@/utils";`:

   ```ts
   import { useTwoFactorLogin } from "../-hooks/use-two-factor-login";
   ```

3. Replace `const loginMutation = useLogin();` with:

   ```ts
   const loginMutation = useTwoFactorLogin();
   ```

Nothing else in the form changes: `loginMutation.mutate(data, { onSuccess, onError })`
keeps its current callbacks.

## Manual step: add the i18n keys

`t()` keys are type-checked against `src/i18n/locales/en.json`, and none of these
exist in the boilerplate yet. Add them to `src/i18n/locales/en.json` (and translate
them in `src/i18n/locales/es.json`).

Under `form` (`schemas.ts` and the forms read `form.otp` and `form.recoveryCode`):

```json
"otp": "One-time code",
"recoveryCode": "Recovery code"
```

As a new top-level key:

```json
"twoFactor": {
  "backToLogin": "Back to sign in",
  "verify": {
    "title": "Two-factor authentication",
    "oneTimePasswordDescription": "Enter the 6-digit code from your authenticator app.",
    "recoveryCodeDescription": "Enter one of the recovery codes you saved when you set up two-factor authentication.",
    "useRecoveryCode": "Use a recovery code instead",
    "useOneTimePassword": "Use your authenticator app instead",
    "submit": "Verify"
  },
  "setup": {
    "title": "Set up two-factor authentication",
    "loading": "Preparing your setup...",
    "loadError": "We couldn't start the setup. Sign in again to retry.",
    "scanQrCode": "Scan this QR code with your authenticator app.",
    "qrCodeAlt": "QR code for your authenticator app",
    "manualEntryKey": "Or enter this key manually:",
    "recoveryCodesTitle": "Recovery codes",
    "recoveryCodesDescription": "Save these codes somewhere safe. Each one signs you in once if you lose your device. They won't be shown again.",
    "copyRecoveryCodes": "Copy recovery codes",
    "recoveryCodesCopied": "Recovery codes copied",
    "recoveryCodesNotCopied": "Couldn't copy the recovery codes. Copy them by hand.",
    "confirmDescription": "Enter the 6-digit code from your authenticator app to finish."
  },
  "errors": {
    "invalidCode": "That code didn't work. Try again.",
    "tooManyAttempts": "Too many attempts. Wait a few minutes and try again.",
    "expired": "Your verification expired. Sign in again.",
    "unexpected": "Something went wrong. Try again in a moment."
  }
}
```

The screens also reuse `login.success`, which the boilerplate already has.

## API contract

| Function | Path | Auth | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `loginWithTwoFactorChallenge` | `{{ loginEndpoint }}` | credentials | Returns the challenge, or `null` when the backend logged the user in directly |
| `setupTwoFactor` | `{{ twoFactorSetupEndpoint }}` | challenge token | Returns the QR SVG, the manual-entry secret, and the recovery codes |
| `completeTwoFactor` | `{{ twoFactorCompleteEndpoint }}` | challenge token | Serves both setup-confirm and login-verify; creates the session and returns the user |
| `verifyRecoveryCode` | `{{ twoFactorVerifyRecoveryCodeEndpoint }}` | challenge token | Lost-device path; creates the session and returns the user, same as `completeTwoFactor` |
| `requestTwoFactorReset` | `{{ twoFactorRequestResetEndpoint }}` | cookie session | Re-validates the password before issuing a reset challenge token |
| `resetTwoFactor` | `{{ twoFactorResetEndpoint }}` | reset challenge token | Clears the secret; the user re-enrolls through setup afterward |
| `disableTwoFactor` | `{{ twoFactorDisableEndpoint }}` | cookie session | Fails with 403 when `google2fa.mandatory` is `true` on the backend - surface that error, don't hide the button |
| `regenerateRecoveryCodes` | `{{ twoFactorRegenerateRecoveryCodesEndpoint }}` | cookie session | No dedicated screen shipped; wire it into wherever your settings page lives |

Two of these paths group differently from what you might expect from a similar
existing project: recovery-code verification and regeneration live under
`/2fa/*` rather than under `/auth/*`, per `docs/google-2fa.md` - the routes stub
this installer wired for you follows that grouping, so the paths above are the
ones that are actually live once `two-factor-auth.php` is required.

## Screens still to build

- **Disable 2FA (self-service)** - a password-confirmation form calling
  `disableTwoFactor`, handling the 403 `mandatory` case above instead of assuming
  the action always succeeds.
- **Regenerate recovery codes** - a password-confirmation form calling
  `regenerateRecoveryCodes`.
- **Reset flow** - `requestTwoFactorReset` (password confirm, while still holding
  a cookie session) followed by `resetTwoFactor` (spends the resulting challenge
  token) is a two-step flow; design it to fit your own account-recovery patterns.

